('MER-REL-001','Resilience','Service criticality','Every material service shall have a criticality classification based on harm from disruption, including customer, safety, financial, market, legal and systemic impact.'),
('MER-REL-002','Resilience','Impact tolerance','Critical services shall define maximum tolerable disruption and supporting recovery objectives in business terms, then map dependencies needed to remain within them.'),
('MER-REL-003','Resilience','SLOs','User-facing and enabling services shall define service-level objectives or equivalent measurable reliability targets appropriate to criticality.'),
('MER-REL-004','Resilience','Recovery by test','Recovery capability shall be demonstrated through scheduled restoration, failover or continuity exercises; untested recovery documentation is not sufficient evidence.'),
('MER-REL-005','Resilience','Progressive change','Production change shall use automated testing and, where appropriate, progressive exposure, health checks, rollback or roll-forward mechanisms to limit blast radius.'),
('MER-REL-006','Resilience','Capacity and saturation','Critical services shall monitor capacity, dependency saturation and failure modes with enough headroom or elasticity to meet defined tolerances.'),
('MER-REL-007','Resilience','Incident command','Material incidents shall use a clear incident command model separating technical coordination, business decision-making, communications and evidence capture.'),
('MER-REL-008','Resilience','Learning without blame','Post-incident learning shall focus on system conditions, incentives, controls and recovery opportunities rather than individual blame, while preserving accountability for misconduct.'),
